1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERB: In January 1970, at Britain's G C H Q, James Ellis wrote a secret report on non-secret encryption.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERA: The idea came from a wartime Bell Labs paper, in which the receiver adds noise to a call, then removes it.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERB: So the receiver helps lock the message.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERA: Right. Ellis argued that it was possible, but he couldn't see a way to build it.

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERB: Then, in September 1973, a young mathematician, Clifford Cocks, joined and heard about the idea.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERA: Cocks knew number theory. He chose prime factoring, and quickly found what we now call R S A.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERB: He didn't even write it down at the time.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERA: That's four years before M I T.

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERB: And in 1974, his colleague Malcolm Williamson found what we now call Diffie–Hellman key exchange.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERA: With a military focus and little computing power, G C H Q found no use for it, and it stayed secret.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERA: It was made public in nineteen ninety-seven, when Cocks gave a talk on the eighteenth of December.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERB: James Ellis had died on the 25th of November, a month too soon.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: And as far as anyone knows, the Stanford and M I T teams found it all on their own.

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERB: So what actually happens when you open a secure web page today?

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: Your browser and the server run a fresh key exchange, often on elliptic curves rather than a clock.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: The server signs the exchange, and its certificate shows that the signature really is the site's.

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: That's the answer to Mallory.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERB: Public-key maths is slow, so it only sets up a key; a fast ordinary cipher carries the page.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERA: And the exchange keys are thrown away afterwards, so a key stolen later can't unlock today's traffic.

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERB: R S A keys usually have 2,048 bits or more. On elliptic curves, 256 bits does as well as 3,072.
